            <div class="prose prose-lg text-gray-700">

                <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 mb-8">Metro Nashville Police Department’s September 2, 2026 release is blunt: most guns reported stolen in Nashville this year were taken from vehicles. The department strongly encourages Nashvillians and visitors to lock their automobile doors, secure any valuables — especially guns — and remove the keys.</p>

                <p>This is not a rewrite of the after-theft steps. If a firearm is already gone, start with <a href="stolen-firearm-checklist.html" class="text-av-gold hover:underline">What to Do When a Firearm Is Stolen</a>. This post is about a different records problem than a safe that left the house or a later recovery: a gun left in a car, and a list that should not be sitting in the same glovebox.</p>

                <h2 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold text-gray-900">Nashville, 2026: most stolen guns came from vehicles</h2>
                <p>According to a <a href="https://www.nashville.gov/departments/police/news/majority-guns-reported-stolen-nashville-taken-vehicles-71" class="text-av-gold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">September 2 release</a> from the Metropolitan Nashville Police Department:</p>
                <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                    <li>So far in 2026, 436 guns have been stolen from vehicles in Nashville.</li>
                    <li>17 were taken from autos the week before that release.</li>
                    <li>The total number of guns stolen this year in Davidson County is 641.</li>
                    <li>That means 68% of guns stolen so far in 2026 have been taken from automobiles.</li>
                    <li>Last year at this same time, 655 guns had been stolen from vehicles — 219 more than this year. The vehicle-theft count is down, not gone.</li>
                </ul>

                <p>The release does not describe recoveries. It does not name victims. It does not say anyone handed over serials. What it does say is where the thefts are happening, and what MNPD wants people to do about the cars: lock the doors, secure valuables (especially guns), and take the keys.</p>

                <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-xl p-6 my-8">
                    <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
                        <i class="bi bi-lightbulb text-amber-600 text-xl mt-1"></i>
                        <div>
                            <h4 class="font-semibold text-amber-900 mb-1">Lock the car. Keep a copy that is not in it.</h4>
                            <p class="text-amber-800 text-sm mb-0">MNPD’s advice is about the vehicle. A list of serials in the glovebox, a printed inventory in the console, or a notebook under the seat leaves with the same car. If the car is hit, you still need make, model, caliber, and serials you can open from somewhere else.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h2 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold text-gray-900">Don't leave the only list in the glovebox</h2>
                <p>A locked car is still the right place to start — that is what MNPD is asking for. It is the wrong place for the only copy of the serials.</p>

                <p>If someone breaks a window or takes the vehicle, you need the same identifiers police need for any other stolen-firearm report: make, model, caliber, and serial number. Those numbers do not stop the theft. They are what you can still hand over after the car has been hit.</p>

                <p>A list that lives only in the vehicle is the vehicle version of a list that lives only in the safe. This post is not <a href="when-the-safe-gets-stolen.html" class="text-av-gold hover:underline">Your Safe Left the House. Do You Still Have the Serials?</a> Those thefts are about a safe that left or was found open. This one is about guns left in automobiles.</p>

                <p>When guns leave a cabinet or a house instead of a car, police still need a specific list. On September 3, 2026, the Saline County Sheriff’s Office, as reported by <a href="https://www.ksal.com/guns-stolen-in-burglary/" class="text-av-gold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">KSAL</a>, investigated a residential burglary in the 4100 Block of N Link Road: forced entry through a back door, a gun cabinet broken into, and multiple Remingtons listed by model — rifles and shotguns, some with scopes — with total loss and damage valued at $3,400. The cabinet did not hand over that list. Neither will an empty passenger seat.</p>

                <h2 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold text-gray-900">What police need the same day</h2>
                <p>When you call, the useful record is specific. “A gun from the car” is a start. Make, model, caliber, and serial number is what turns that start into an entry in a crime database.</p>

                <div class="bg-gray-100 rounded-xl p-6 my-8">
                    <h4 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-4"><i class="bi bi-check2-square text-av-gold mr-2"></i>Have this ready before you need it</h4>
                    <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700">
                        <li><i class="bi bi-check text-av-gold mr-2"></i> Make / manufacturer</li>
                        <li><i class="bi bi-check text-av-gold mr-2"></i> Model</li>
                        <li><i class="bi bi-check text-av-gold mr-2"></i> Caliber or gauge</li>
                        <li><i class="bi bi-check text-av-gold mr-2"></i> <strong>Serial number</strong></li>
                        <li><i class="bi bi-check text-av-gold mr-2"></i> Photos, if you have them — overall and serial close-up</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <p>That is the same-day list. Distinguishing marks and accessories help, but they are secondary to being able to identify each firearm. Check your local reporting rules; some jurisdictions set a deadline. This is not legal advice — use local law and the <a href="stolen-firearm-checklist.html" class="text-av-gold hover:underline">stolen-firearm checklist</a> for the after-theft sequence.</p>

                <p>If those guns later turn up, matching them back to you is still serial-number work. That is <a href="when-stolen-guns-come-back.html" class="text-av-gold hover:underline">When Stolen Guns Come Back, Matching Them to You Takes Serials</a> — recovery is possible; matching is a different job. This post does not claim Nashville recovered anything.</p>

                <h2 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold text-gray-900">How Arsenal Vault helps</h2>
                <p>Arsenal Vault is a hosted inventory: serials, photos, values, and documents you can open from another device and export when something happens. Your data. Your control. Your vault.</p>

                <p>The point is not more software. The point is a copy you can still open if the car was just hit — so police have identifiers to enter, and so you are not reconstructing serials from memory in a parking lot.</p>

